# docker/pgbouncer/userlist.txt.example
#
# Copy this to docker/pgbouncer/userlist.txt (gitignored - never commit
# the real file) and replace the placeholder with the PLAINTEXT password
# you set for the `pgbouncer_auth` role with:
#   ALTER ROLE pgbouncer_auth WITH PASSWORD '<a real secret>';
# (role created by docker/postgres/initdb/01_pgbouncer_auth.sql).
#
# This is the ONLY credential that belongs in this file. PgBouncer uses
# it for two things, both as pgbouncer_auth:
#   1. to open its own connection to Postgres and run auth_query, and
#   2. to let you log into the admin console (SHOW POOLS, etc.).
# App user passwords are looked up dynamically via auth_query and are
# never written here.
#
# Why plaintext and NOT the SCRAM verifier from pg_shadow: PgBouncer is
# the *client* on its connection to Postgres, and a SCRAM client needs
# the real password to complete the handshake. The stored verifier
# (what pg_shadow holds) lacks the ClientKey, so PgBouncer can verify
# other people with it but cannot authenticate itself with it. The
# symptom of using the verifier is the app failing to log in while
# `SHOW POOLS` still works (the admin console is virtual and never
# opens that backend connection).
#
# Edit this file by hand - there is no generator target. Format:
#   "username" "password"
# Avoid double quotes inside the password (or double them: "" ).

"pgbouncer_auth" "REPLACE_WITH_PGBOUNCER_AUTH_PASSWORD"
